WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.800
1. Start with a rectangle with 1:2 proportions.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.000
Don't make a square flag. Looking at you, Vatican City.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
2. Use simple shapes and maybe a silhouette to create symbolism for what the flag represents.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.500
Never use seals or complex designs that can't be drawn by a child in several seconds.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.140
3. Keep the design simple, while still differentiating it from other flags.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.400
4. Use 2 or 3 colors that look good together.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.500
You may want to consider using lighter colors, which tend to look better.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.199
5. A good flag is recognizable backwards, and in many other scenarios.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.000
One trick to make sure a flag can be recognized

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
even when sitting still on a pole, rather than it flying in the wind,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.800
is to make a unique design element in the canton, the top left corner.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.709
Seem familiar? Well, probably because it is.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.800
I hope you now know a little bit more about vexillology.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.040
If you found this interesting, follow the links in the notes for some cool related pages and sites. Good day!

